From e871f32be095fa2d07eb422d5d37f20193c71b5f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sun, 26 Jul 2026 20:04:37 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 07990/11362] [outer-loop] gateway-accurate re-scoring: the
 uncharged-RESET optimism is 4.6% (measured), not the 0-96% the bound allowed

THE DEFECT. The live gateway charges a RESET one action (arc_agi/scorecard.py:701-704
inc_reset_count -> resets+=1 AND actions+=1, via update_scorecard:839-843). Our offline harness
charged it ZERO (actions+=1 lived only in the non-RESET branch). Since the scorer's per-level
cost is a DIFFERENCE of cumulative CHARGED counts (:479) and the per-level score is
min((baseline/level_actions)^2*100, 115), every per-level efficiency number this project holds
was optimistic in the SQUARED term by the resets charged before that level-up. Magnitude was
unmeasured.

WHAT THE BOUND COULD ANSWER: NOTHING. The 1401 persisted rows record only a whole-run n_resets,
so the correction is bounded, not determined: worst case erases 0%-95.7% of a cell's score
(median 11.4%) and the best case is 0% BY CONSTRUCTION. Reported as an honest "too wide to say"
rather than dressed up -- and that is precisely what justified instrumenting.

WHAT THE EXACT MEASUREMENT ANSWERS. run_game now records per-level reset attribution
(resets_before_levelups / level_up_charged / efficiency_gateway_charged). 44 MATCHED live cells
(11 games x 3 seeds x b400+b2000, LLM-off, same seeding) give: median 4.62% of the per-game
score, max 16.80%, 42/44 non-zero. On the GAME as the replication unit (n=11): median 4.92%,
range 1.81%-16.66% -- same answer, so it does not rest on which games repeated. MATERIAL BUT
SMALL; no settled conclusion is reordered (depth still dominates, per-game score still cannot
exceed 100, post-solve tail still free).

WHY reality sits at the optimistic end: level-ups happen early and resets pile up in the FREE
post-solve tail (vc33 b2000: 1 of 88 resets precede a level-up). The correction tracks
resets-BEFORE-the-levelup, not n_resets -- r11l (72 of 109 before) is hit 4x harder than ft09
(4 of 110). That dose-response is why the n_resets-only bound was uninformative.

MATCHED-DESIGN WITNESS: all 15 b400 cells reproduce their recorded row's efficiency AND
n_resets exactly, so the gateway numbers attach to the recorded corpus rather than describing a
different agent.

SCORER FIDELITY, not a paraphrase: two independent drivers of the INSTALLED scorer --
EnvironmentScoreCalculator as _calculate_score drives it, and a real Scorecard/Card built
through the REAL mutators then scored via from_scorecard -- agree on 87/87 distinct
(game,budget,levels,per-level-cost) signatures. The full chain also re-derives both settled
facts: a reset IS charged, and the post-solve tail IS free.

FOUR MEASUREMENT TRAPS CAUGHT IN MY OWN WORK, not waved through:
1. GREEDY IS WRONG HERE. Greedy-on-marginals disagrees with the exact DP on 7/485 cells, always
   understating the damage: a level solved SUPERHUMANLY sits at the 115 cap, whose flat region
   has zero marginal, so greedy refuses to spend into it (real sp80 cell: 27 agent vs 39 human
   actions -> raw 208.6, capped). Pinned as a regression test.
2. ROUNDING MANUFACTURED A FALSE NULL. run_game rounds efficiency to 4dp, which turned 2 real
   deltas into exact 0.0000 (ft09 truly 1.81%, s5i5 truly 3.85%). Every statistic is recomputed
   UNROUNDED; the only 2 structural zeros are the cap absorbing a single reset.
3. A LARGER DEFECT RULED OUT FROM SOURCE. The gateway consumes actions_by_level POSITIONALLY
   (:477 ignores the stored level number), so a RESET that dropped levels_completed would shift
   every later level's charge. arcengine/base_game.py:308-328: a mid-run reset is level_reset(),
   which does NOT touch _score. Does not materialize; the residual full_reset edge case is
   stated, not hidden.
4. NON-INDEPENDENT CELLS. tu93 returns byte-identical results across all 3 seeds, so its 3 seeds
   are 1 observation. Per-game units reported beside per-cell.

INDEPENDENT CORROBORATION: a concurrent session's separately-written implementation
(analyze_arc_reset_charge_attribution.py, different worst-case search) agrees 7/7 on corpus
size, bounded-cell count, the frames identity, the upstream clock, and the whole worst-case loss
distribution (median 0.113725, max 0.956551). Its max_weights-clamp qualifier is adopted here as
a second reason the exact correction lands optimistic.

REALITY CROSS-CHECK: DOES NOT CORROBORATE, AND IS UNDERPOWERED. The offline PUBLIC-set
mean-per-game (0.088) is already near the actual HIDDEN 0.08; the correction moves it DOWN to
0.057, AWAY from the observed value. Stated as a non-explanation rather than forced: different
game sets, different dominant term (hidden score is gated by first_win_rate 0.04 -- winning
NOTHING -- which no efficiency correction can move), different LLM configuration, and one
uninterval'd observation.

COVERAGE HOLE NAMED: 1713 cptb cells + 375 budget-sweep rows declare
action_count_convention: resets_excluded_run_game_native but record NO reset count, so they
CANNOT be re-scored at any width.

CLOCK HYGIENE: Part A publishes measurement_wall_s = 9126.0s (sum of upstream row files' own
elapsed_s, not per-cell wall_s which undercounts ~25%); Part B publishes its own
part_b_measurement_wall_s = 523.5s; duration_s (1.2s) is labelled as the ANALYSER clock only.

ALSO FIXED (both found by their own guards firing correctly):
- summarize_artifact.py:230 CRASHED with AttributeError on a list-shaped
