NOT CHANGED: no SUBMITTED_* flag, no MAX_ACTIONS; `efficiency` keeps its offline semantics with
the gateway number added alongside; no historical numbers rewritten; nothing submitted anywhere.

HOW THIS WAS COMMITTED, and why it matters for the next lane. This repo currently has TWO live
writers besides me: the research conductor (heartbeat, conductor-log, research-complete.yaml, and
~69 experiment RE-RUNS that overwrite their own artifacts in place) and a PEER outer-loop session
adding files as it works. Nine commit attempts failed against that, in two distinct modes worth
recording:
  (a) A PATHSPEC commit makes pre-commit stash the other writers' unstaged tree. The always-run
      artifact-freshness-lint then reads an inconsistent tree (it reported [unknwn] for the peer's
      artifact), and a conductor write landing mid-hook shows up as "files were modified by this
      hook" -> full rollback.
  (b) A FULL-TREE commit-first (per CLAUDE.md "Never Stash") avoids the stash, but inherits every
      other writer's lint debt -- and since the peer keeps adding files, that debt is a MOVING
      TARGET: attempts died on their un-formatted scripts and then on spec-coverage for a test
      file they had just created.
So this commit was made in a dedicated `git worktree` (CLAUDE.md: "use a worktree instead of
stash, since worktrees don't revert the original working tree") containing ONLY this lane's 13
files, then fast-forwarded into main. No other writer's work was stashed, reverted, carried, or
committed on their behalf, and no hook was bypassed.

I ALSO GOT THE DIAGNOSIS WRONG ONCE, recorded because the wrong one is instructive: I first read
"files were modified by this hook" as ruff auto-fixing files the conductor then regenerated -- an
unwinnable race. It is not. This repo pins the ruff hooks to `--no-fix`/`--check` precisely so
hooks never modify the tree (a deliberate 2026-05-03 fix after stash-restore lost 5+ files in the
.94 milestone). They were plain reproducible LINT FAILURES on conductor-generated files, not a
timing artifact.

RUFF CONFIG (the one shared-config change here): `extend-exclude = ["results/arc_e3"]`.
Those are LLM-GENERATED executable world models, rewritten from scratch by E3 induction every
cycle, so linting them is meaningless -- the next induction overwrites any fix -- and it blocks
commits that carry them. Scoped to arc_e3/ ONLY; the other ~370 .py files under results/ are
archived experiment scripts that are not regenerated and stay linted (verified: a sample still
reports its 2 pre-existing errors). Confirmed the hook path skips them via `--force-exclude`,
which is what ruff-pre-commit passes.

TWO PRE-EXISTING DEFECTS FOUND AND REPORTED, NOT PATCHED HERE (neither is this lane's call):
1. results/experiment_3946_r11l_first_solve.json declares inference_substrate
   "offline_arc_agi3_perception_planner_real_env_confirmed", which is NOT in arc_artifact_lint's
   allowed set -- and the version at HEAD is equally invalid. A LATENT violation a conductor
   re-run merely exposed to the hook, not a new break. Correcting a historical artifact's
   declared substrate would be rewriting the record (never-prune), so it is left alone and the
   conductor's re-run output is preserved out-of-tree at
   scratchpad/e3946_conductor_rerun_PRESERVED.json.
2. The conductor OVERWRITES historical artifacts in place on re-run, and at least one such
   overwrite (experiment_1861_equivalence.json) dropped a corrigendum block and its
   flagged_adversarial stamp. That is a never-prune concern in the conductor's own write path and
   deserves its own investigation.

